{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"What actually happens (experiments on a99339e8ce8, stateful fake Jira, scratch test not committed): (1) A refused inbound EDIT ends the pass before any outbound write (applier applies inbound before outbound); the next pass imports the change with no Jira writes. Delayed, not lost. (2) A refusal in a pass that also had a local-origin outbound create made no Jira write that pass; the next pass created DIG-100 once, bound it, and later passes made no writes. No duplicate. (3) LOST: new Jira issue DIG-2's inbound create landed its Jira writeback (label rebar-id:jira-dig-2, property local_id) before a later inbound write was refused. With the pass's uncommitted files discarded (the production bridge model), 5 later passes never import DIG-2: no local ticket, no binding, no Jira writes. With the files kept (persistent clone), the next pass converges. The same loss follows a RuntimeError at that point, so the reclaim refusal is one trigger of a general defect. Outcome: not the faulty-premise state; fix needed."},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1795270912608049001,"uuid":"1eb78ddf-b9d7-4284-9623-300da414ee54"}